#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Dot relay: Slack channel and allowlisted Mac command relay (no model calls).

SETUP (operator-owned; this script never creates or requests credentials):
  Reuses Hermes' mode-600, user-owned ~/.hermes/.env OUTSIDE the repository.
  Put literal dotenv values there, without shell substitutions:
    SLACK_USER_TOKEN   Slack user OAuth token for channel threads; chat:write
                      plus channels:history (public) / groups:history (private).
    SLACK_BOT_TOKEN    Alternative bot OAuth token for DM threads only;
                      chat:write plus im:history / mpim:history as applicable.
    SLACK_HOME_CHANNEL Existing destination convention: conversation ID, not
                      a workspace ID. The token's principal must be a member.
    DOT_SLACK_SENDER   Exact trusted Dot reply user ID or bot ID. Other senders,
                      message edits, and deleted messages cannot run commands.
    DOT_SLACK_MENTION  Optional literal mention prefixed to each brief, if the
                      agent requires one. Connect the agent to that channel.
  No app-level token, Socket Mode, model token, or environment token is used.
  Reuses installed slack_sdk if available; stdlib HTTP otherwise. No installs.
  Confirm one live Dot reply first: merely connecting Slack in ChatGPT does
  not establish an agent subscription to channel messages.

USAGE (global options precede the subcommand):
  bin/dot-relay send-job /path/to/brief.txt
  bin/dot-relay watch <thread-ts>             # polling only; no commands run
  bin/dot-relay relay <thread-ts>             # polling + allowlisted execution
  send-job accepts --cwd <repo-subdir> or --scratch (mktemp owned by this job).
  watch/relay poll every 30s; errors back off to 300s and honor Retry-After.
  --max-polls bounds a run (default 720); exit 2 means no final report received.

PROTOCOL:
  Long briefs post as a root message plus in-thread continuation parts;
  send-job returns the root timestamp used for Dot replies. Multipart roots
  say to wait; the final continuation ends with DOT-BRIEF-END. Dot must wait
  for that final part before requesting commands or reporting completion.
  Replies at or before the persisted final-part timestamp are ignored.
  Slack writes are paced at one per second; ambiguous writes are never retried.
  In the same Slack thread, the trusted sender requests one command per line:
    ```mac-run
    git status --short
    head -n 30 lib/example.py
    ```
  Commands use the job cwd (default ~/carr-system); no shell or cd syntax.
  Narrow argv grammar supports read-only git fetch/log/show/diff/status/
  ls-files/grep, cat/head/sed -n/grep/rg/ls/wc, python3 -m pytest, named
  tools/test-*.py or ops/*-selftest.py, and ./ops/ci.sh --only unit. Other
  options, path traversal, hidden paths, escapes and substitutions are held.
  Test scripts execute repository code; this is an argv/path boundary, not an
  OS sandbox. It assumes the checked-out source and git origin are trusted.
  Git's discovered worktree and administrative directories must stay inside
  approved roots; an external linked worktree or enclosing repository is held.
  Output is redacted, limited to 150 lines / 12000 bytes, and posted as plain
  text in-thread. Each command times out after 60s; exit 124 denotes timeout,
  125 output limit. Refused/interrupted requests post "held for orchestrator".
  Outside any fence, DOT-REPORT-END ends the report alone on a stripped line
  or as a whitespace-separated suffix, optionally followed by one space and
  a job label matching [A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,48}. Text before the suffix becomes the
  last report line. Mid-sentence mentions do not finish. Completion saves
  that reply's report and exits 0.
  Watch saves a report without consuming commands needed by a later relay.
  Outgoing message timestamps are persisted and excluded from inbound control.

STATE:
  ~/.local/state/dot-relay/<thread-ts>/ contains job.json, state.json,
  ledger.jsonl ({ts, command, cwd, exit, allowed, bytes}), pending.jsonl and
  report.txt; files are mode 600, directories 700, outside the repository.
  --state-dir and --credentials override those paths for an operator setup.
  A lock prevents concurrent execution. Durable claims prevent replay after
  interruption; inspect pending.jsonl before requesting a NEW command. Posts
  have no automatic write retry: reconcile an ambiguous send against Slack.
  A pending root claim in the state directory's send-job.json blocks new jobs
  and polling until reconciled after the sender releases its lock. Healthy
  active root sends leave other job watchers running. Continuation claims and
  the final brief timestamp live in thread state.json.
  An interrupted result post also stops resume until private state is reconciled.
  Never commit local state, reports, credentials or live message contents.

API references:
  https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/conversations.replies/
  https://docs.slack.dev/reference/methods/chat.postMessage/
"""
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]))
from lib.dot_relay import main

if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
